The operational dashboard should make gaps obvious:

EV OCPI HEALTHY
NESO TEC HEALTHY
Mouser HEALTHY
Apprentices HEALTHY
Fuel Finder FAILED

Collector reliability is part of the moat. A year of history with
unexplained holes is much less valuable.
10. Storage efficiency

For large feeds that barely change:

poll
=E2=86=92 hash

same hash:
record successful poll
do not duplicate giant payload

new hash:
store new raw payload
emit change

For truly dynamic feeds such as EV status:

EVSE_ID | timestamp | status

Store a compact continuous state tape plus periodic complete raw snapshots
for forensic recovery.
11. Prioritize by irreversibility CLASS A =E2=80=94 COLLECT NOW

State disappears and cannot be cheaply reconstructed later.

   - EV live availability
   - component inventory
   - component lead time
   - marketplace state
   - dynamic tariffs
   - grid outages
   - connection queue snapshots
   - apprenticeship adverts
   - compute economics
   - supplier availability

CLASS B =E2=80=94 COLLECT SOON

Some history exists, but not necessarily our exact state tape.

   - Companies House stream
   - OPSS recalls
   - installer registers
   - planning changes
   - training-provider state

CLASS C =E2=80=94 INGEST WHEN NEEDED

Historical backfill already exists.

   - MOT bulk
   - EPC
   - Land Registry
   - historic Elexon
   - Open Repair Alliance
   - OpenWrt
   - PCI IDs

Engineering priority should follow irreversibility, not ease of ingestion.

MOT can wait because it can be downloaded later.

Yesterday=E2=80=99s charger status cannot.
